pinctrl: sunxi: h713: use EINT function 0xe and mux IRQ pins again

The H713 pin tables were derived from the H616 and carried its EINT mux
value 0x6. On the H713 -- new register layout like the D1 -- the EINT
function is 0xe: the vendor ARISC firmware programs PL4 to function 14
for the power key, and measured on the HY310 (2026-09-09) PL4 at mux 0
toggles the data bit but never raises EINT4 across many presses, while
mux 0xe raises it on every edge. Patch 0004 had stopped switching the
mux on IRQ request because 0x6 "disconnected the input"; that was the
wrong value, not a property of the SoC. Set 0xe in both tables and
switch the pin to the EINT function on request like upstream does.
Only IRQ 265 (gpio-keys "power") uses a GPIO interrupt on this board.

Plan doku/103 G3, report S38 section 7.

--- a/drivers/pinctrl/sunxi/pinctrl-sunxi.c	2026-09-09 18:25:42.531053342 +0200
+++ b/drivers/pinctrl/sunxi/pinctrl-sunxi.c	2026-09-09 18:25:42.540537134 +0200
@@ -1054,13 +1054,16 @@
 		return ret;
 	}
 
-	/* Change muxing to INT mode.
-	 * Some SoCs (e.g. H713) can detect edges in GPIO mode and setting
-	 * the IRQ mux function actually disconnects the input path.
-	 * Skip mux change when irq_read_needs_mux is false.
+	/*
+	 * Change muxing to INT mode. The H713 does not detect edges in GPIO
+	 * mode: with PL4 left at mux 0 the power key toggled the data bit
+	 * but never raised EINT4, with mux 0xe every edge did (HY310,
+	 * 2026-09-09). The earlier "edges work in GPIO mode" workaround only
+	 * looked that way because the tables carried the H616 value 0x6,
+	 * which is not the EINT function on this SoC and disconnected the
+	 * input; the tables now say 0xe, like the D1's.
 	 */
-	if (pctl->desc->irq_read_needs_mux)
-		sunxi_pmx_set(pctl->pctl_dev, pctl->irq_array[d->hwirq], func->muxval);
+	sunxi_pmx_set(pctl->pctl_dev, pctl->irq_array[d->hwirq], func->muxval);
 
 	return 0;
 }
